"color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%;padding-left:9.67pt"&gt;Long-Term Debt and Revolving Credit Facilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Bank Credit Facilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company maintains a credit agreement which allows for a credit facility borrowing capacity of $300.0 million. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company is permitted to increase the size of its revolving credit facility by up to $50.0 million, subject to the satisfaction of certain conditions. This expansion feature is uncommitted. The revolving credit facility permits borrowings at Term SOFR (Secured Overnight Financing Rate) plus a margin of 0.85% to 1.35% or an alternate base rate plus a margin of 0.00% to 0.35%. The revolving credit facility also has annual commitment fees ranging from 0.06% to 0.175%. Interest rates and commitment fees are based on the Company&#8217;s then-current senior debt rating. A portion of the revolving credit facility not in excess of $65.0 million is available for the issuance of letters of credit (LOCs).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The credit agreement contains a covenant package consistent with investment grade companies. As of March 31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, the Company was in compliance with these covenants.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;As of March 31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, the Company had $205.0 and $70.0 million of revolver borrowings under the revolving credit facility, respectively. As of March 31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, the Company had no current debt maturities, $0.5 million of outstanding LOCs issued under the revolving credit facility and no outstanding LOCs issued under standalone facilities unrelated to the revolving credit facility, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;First Mortgage Bonds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On April 30, 2025, the Company closed and funded $200.0 million of first mortgage bonds in a private placement offering. The offering includes 2 tranches &#8211; (1) $50.0 million of 7-year first mortgage bonds which bear interest at a rate of 5.21% and mature on April 30, 2032 and (2) $150.0 million of 10-year first mortgage bonds which bear interest at a rate of 5.44% and mature on April 30, 2035. The proceeds of this issuance were used to repay existing indebtedness and for general corporate purposes, including capital expenditures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;On April 28, 2026, DLC priced $180.0 million of first mortgage bonds in a private placement offering. The first mortgage bonds will bear interest at a rate of 5.26% and mature on June 15, 2036. The proceeds of this issuance, which are to be closed and funded on June 15, 2026, will be used to repay existing indebtedness and for general corporate purposes, including capital expenditures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Short-Term Borrowing Affiliated Interest Agreement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;An existing PUC approved affiliated interest agreement is maintained between the Company and Holdings, which authorizes short-term borrowings at market rates from Holdings with a maximum borrowing capacity of $300.0 million. In May 2023, the PUC approved the Company&#8217;s request to amend this agreement, changing the benchmark interest rate from London Inter-bank Offered Rate to SOFR. No other aspects of the agreement were amended. As of March 31, 2026 $100.0 million in borrowings were outstanding under this agreement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Securities Certificates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;In September 2024, the FERC approved Duquesne Light&#8217;s application for a securities certificate requesting approval to issue short-term securities in an amount not to exceed $650.0 million. As of March 31, 2026, Duquesne Light had $305.0 million of borrowings outstanding under this order. This amount is $100.0 million of short-term intercompany borrowings between the Company and Holdings and the Company&#8217;s $205.0 million in revolver borrowings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;In October 2025 the PUC approved Duquesne Light&#8217;s application for a new securities certificate allowing Duquesne Light to issue up to $400.0 million of long-term debt in the form of first mortgage bonds, unsecured notes, bank borrowings or similar borrowings through December 31, 2027. As of March 31, 2026, $400.0 million of long-term debt was available for issuance under this securities certificate.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%;padding-left:9.67pt"&gt;Commitments and Contingencies &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:109%"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Currently, the Company is not subject to any federal or state income tax examinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;The Company remains subject to examination by federal and state tax authorities for tax years ending December 31, 2022 through December 31, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:109%"&gt;Employees &#8211; IBEW Contract&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company is a party to a labor contract with the IBEW Local 29, which represents approximately 50.0% of the approximately 1,800 employees. In October 2023, members of the IBEW ratified a new four year labor contract extension that commenced on October 1, 2023 and expires on September 30, 2026. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:109%"&gt;Litigation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt; In the ordinary course of business, various legal claims and proceedings are pending or threatened against the Company. While the amounts claimed may be substantial, the Company is unable to predict with certainty the ultimate outcome of such claims and proceedings. The Company has established reserves for pending litigation, which it believes are adequate, and after consultation with counsel and giving appropriate consideration to available insurance, the Company believes that the ultimate outcome of any matter currently pending will not materially affect the financial statements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:18pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%;padding-left:9.67pt"&gt;Employee Benefits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Pension Benefits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company maintains a qualified retirement plan that provides pension benefits to all eligible full-time employees hired before October 1, 2010. Upon retirement, an eligible employee receives a monthly pension based on his or her length of service and compensation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The cost of funding the pension plan is determined by the unit credit actuarial cost method. Pension cost (benefit) to expense or capital were approximately $1.6 million and $2.1 million for the nine months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively. The Company is required to establish a regulatory asset or regulatory liability for the difference between the amount of retirement plan expense collected in rates and the amount of retirement plan expenses incurred. This amount is recorded in other operating and maintenance or investment and other income in the statements of operations. The cost recorded to investment and other income was approximately $0.4 million for the three months ended March 30, 2026 and 2025. The actual cost recognized in the statements of operations was approximately $0.8 million for the three months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company intends to fund the pension plan by an amount that is at least equal to the minimum funding requirements of the Pension Protection Act of 2006, but which does not exceed the maximum tax-deductible amount for the year.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company made no voluntary pension plan contributions in the three months ended March 31, 2026 and 2025, respectively. Although management continues to assess additional contribution levels, the Company is currently not required to further contribute to the pension plan in 2026.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Under the terms of the Company&#8217;s rate case settlement, approved by the PUC in August 2024, the Company is required to contribute an average of $10.0 million annually to the pension plan until the Company&#8217;s next rate proceeding. Should the Company conclude that a contribution of less than $10.0 million (as prescribed in the rate case settlement) to the pension plan is appropriate, the Company may reduce the pension contribution and will &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;record a regulatory liability that is equal to 50% of the reduction of the contribution below the level of $10.0 million. Any regulatory liability recorded will be reduced to the extent of 50% of the contributions in excess of $10.0 million in subsequent years. If a regulatory liability remains at the time of the Company&#8217;s next rate proceeding, the regulatory amount will be refunded to rate payers as directed in the next rate proceeding. Any amount recorded as a regulatory liability will not bear an interest obligation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Postretirement Benefits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;In addition to pension benefits, the Company provides certain postretirement plans that provide health care benefits and life insurance for some retired employees that were hired before October 1, 2012. The life insurance plan is non-contributory. Participating retirees make contributions, which may be adjusted annually, to the health care plan. Health care benefits terminate when retirees reach age 65. The Company has the right to modify or terminate the plans. The Company maintains a Voluntary Employees Beneficiary Association (VEBA) trust for a portion of its postretirement obligations. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The Company accrues the actuarially determined costs of the postretirement benefits over the period from the date the employee was hired until the date the employee becomes fully eligible for benefits. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-indent:18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The following table summarizes the components of net periodic costs:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:109%"&gt;Components of Net Periodic Benefit Cost for the Three Months Ended March 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:99.404%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:47.402%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:11.923%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:13.121%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:8.929%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:13.125%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="6" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="6" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Pension&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Postretirement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Pension&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Postretirement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Service cost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1.1&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;0.1&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;1.0&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:1pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align: